\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Zloty soars as crawl cut hopes emerge. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Mai 1998\par 17:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 5 (Reuters) - A member of Poland's monetary council re-ignited hopes of a near-term reduction of the zloty's gradual or crawling devaluation, sending the zloty to a new all-time high on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Monetary Policy Council (RPP) member Marek Dabrowski said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main policy goal of pulling inflation down to the 9.5 percent envisaged in the budget this year was not possible unless the zloty's devaluation factor was reduced further. \par \par \uc2  The 10-member RPP cut the zloty's crawl to 0.8 percent per month against a basket of five Western \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from 1.0 percent in late February, and most analysts did not expect any further significant reductions in the crawl this year. \par \par \uc2  Dabrowski said monthly inflation had to average about 0.4 percent for the rest of 1998 if Poland was to meet its CPI target, which was equal to half of the zloty's monthly crawl. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said even though Dabrowski's views were probably not shared by many of his less outspoken RPP colleagues, his arguments, published in the heavyweight Rzeczpospolita daily, had brought an important issue out into the open. \par \par \uc2  "Monetary authorities stand before an important dilemma: either adjust the exchange rate mechanism so that it enables effective anti-inflationary policy, or revise money supply upward and interest rates downwards," Dabrowski wrote. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said any reduction in the crawling peg would have to be accompanied by a cut in official interest rates to keep foreign capital from flooding Poland's fixed income market and potentially destabilising the economy. \par \par \uc2  "If the crawl is cut then interest rates must follow," said Krzysztof Rybinski, chief analyst at ING Barings in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  But Rybinski added that interest rates would still remain high in real terms to keep the economy from over-heating and again building up inflationary pressure. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the RPP would rather meet the inflation target than keep a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could translate into a crawl cut sometime in the third quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  "My forecast is for the rate of crawl to be cut around September from 0.8 to 0.6 (percent per month) but if there is pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  widens the band then the reduction could happen earlier," said Beshada Madi, vice president at Morgan Stanley in London. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that after inflation fell more than five points to 13.2 percent in 1997, slower price growth had to be maintained to keep inflation expectations on the decline. \par \par \uc2  "It is important to see CPI growth continue to go down, so the economy doesn't stabilise at a high inflation level, making further reductions more difficult," said Jesper Dannesboe, economist at ABN-AMRO in London. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday the zloty hit a record high of 8.31 percent above its targeted basket level - the mid-point of its 20 percent trading band - despite an estimated $100 million dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The zloty topped eight percent above parity for the first time on Monday, ending the day fixed at 8.03 percent. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the zloty could again be pushed into battle with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as early as Wednesday, when \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds a 1.1 billion zloty auction of popular two-and five-year fixed rate T-bonds. \par \par \uc2  "We can expect large demand especially for five-year papers tomorrow as people find Poland's real interest rates extremely attractive," said Ryszard Bublik, fixed income dealer at Nomura International in London. ((Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du550610z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
