\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging market  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  - Asia under a cloud again. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Dolan \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, April 16 (Reuters) - Fresh slippage in emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the focus of Thursday's trading session, standing in contrast to continued strength in U.S. stocks and persistent bullishness in emerging Europe. \par \par \uc2  Renewed weakness in the Japanese yen and a second-quarter reappraisal of regional stock markets by foreign investors has conspired to push emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower too, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit underperformed on Thursday, weighed down by a slide in the local bourse of almost seven percent since last week amid rising concerns about corporate failures and a steep economic slowdown. \par \par \uc2  "Asian equities are going to see a reversal of first-quarter rallies as we start to see the economic pain of the last year's financial shock in the form of bankruptcies, liquidations and unemployment," said Paul MacNamara, emerging markets economist at Julius Baer Investments. \par \par \uc2  "Renewed equity weakness is almost certainly going to feed through to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as well." \par \par \uc2  The failure of this week's meeting of the Group of Seven industrialised nations (G7) to address any concrete proposals for boosting Japan's moribund economy also sent the yen sliding through 130 per dollar overnight and helped boost the dollar across Asia. \par \par \uc2  "People made a lot of money buying Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  baskets in the first quarter of the year and they are basically reluctant to push their luck," said one trader at a U.S. investment bank in London. \par \par \uc2  In emerging Europe, the zloty continued to defy the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pushed higher again on Thursday despite Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap it. \par \par \uc2  Benign March inflation date boosted the Treasury bill market as well and a perception the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is intent on squeezing inflation even further has dampened any speculation of a cut in attractive official interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants interest rates to remain high, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker and more uncertainty in day-to-day dealings. These are conflicting policy aims," said MacNamara at Julius Baer. \par \par \uc2  "The problem is the market still sees this as a one-way bet and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only provides better \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels to buy." \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Russian rouble was steady as Friday's second Duma vote on Sergei Kiriyenko's nomination as Prime Minister was awaited. \par \par \uc2  Kiriyenko was rejected by the Duma last week but President Boris Yeltsin said he will dissolve the lower house if his candidate is rejected three times. \par \par \uc2  The Kremlin denied speculation on Thursday that Yeltsin had second thoughts about his choice of the 35-year old Kiriyenko as premier. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that while the market still remained relatively sanguine about the political hiatus in Moscow, fresh concerns about weakness in world oil prices were probably more of a concern. \par \par \uc2  Statistics out on Thursday showed Russian gross domestic product growth was flat year-on-year in the first quarter of this year, down from a rise of 0.8 percent in the last quarter of 1997. \par \par \uc2  Oil export revenues in Russia are crucial to its budgetary targets this year. Prices firmed on Wednesday and Thursday recovering losses earlier in the week as Venezuelan officials reiterated their commitment to production cuts. \par \par \uc2  The Venezuelan bolivar slipped sharply late Wednesday and has been depreciating in April at a faster rate than in previous months. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MARKETS SNAPSHOT The following is a snapshot of emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates. ASIA\tab   * Indonesian rupiah\tab   7,950 per dollar bid vs 7,800 late in London on Wednesday. * Malaysian ringgit 3.76 per dollar bid vs 3.705 on Wednesday. Thai baht\tab     at 39.95 per dollar vs 39.85. Philippine peso\tab   38.25 per dollar vs 37.9. * South Korean won\tab   1,380 per dollar vs 1,391. * Singapore dollar      1.6015 per dollar versus 1.595. Indian rupee\tab   39.66 per dollar vs 39.70. EUROPE\tab \tab  * Russian rouble\tab   at unchanged at 6.1410 per dollar. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  daily bid/off rates at 6.1100/6.1460 from 6.1090/6.1450 Wednesday. * Mark/Czech crown at 18.635 bid compared with 18.547 Wednesday. Zloty about 7.52 percent above its target basket parity versus 7.38/24 at the open. * Hungarian forint 2.25 percent above basket parity vs 2.25 pct Wednesday. Slovak crown 1.95 percent below basket parity versus 2.05 percent Wednesday. * Ukrainian hryvnia\tab   at 2.0425 per dollar bid versus 2.0435 late Wednesday. Romanian leu\tab   8,378 per dollar vs 8,337. AFRICA      * South African rand\tab   at 5.047 per dollar vs 5.033. Kenyan shilling\tab   59.45 vs 59.45. LATIN AMERICA\tab \tab * Mexican peso \tab  weaker at 8.49 per dollar vs 8.469. Brazil's real\tab   at 1.1405 per dollar bid vs 1.1402. Venezualan bolivar\tab   530.71 vs 529.60. ((London newsroom +44 171 542 6414, fax 583 7239, uk.emergingmarkets.news@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1998.\par 