\par\par \b \uc2  Zloty softens but upward sentiment intact. \b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, March 31 (Reuters) - The zloty softened in dull trade on Tuesday, but dealers said the unit could soon test the psychological barrier at seven percent above the trading band centre if foreign flows into Polish treasuries increased. \par \par \uc2  "The zloty market was quite balanced on Tuesday, but expectations are that the zloty will continue to test its upside in the near term despite a threat of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Wojciech Trzepizur, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at BRE SA. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) staged no \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday after buying an estimated $55 million on Monday when the zloty was at between 6.85 and 6.7 percent above the trading band centre. \par \par \uc2  "The resistance level set by the NBP will continue to be tested and if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is quiet for several days then the zloty will rise again," BRE's Trzepizur said. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed on Tuesday at 6.78/6.74 percent stronger than the parity rate against Monday's 6.81/6.79 percent. \par \par \uc2  It ended at 3.4465/85 to the dollar against 3.4315/25 on Monday and at 1.8671/76 to the mark against 1.8720/25. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said large foreign interest at Wednesday's primary fixed rate note auctions could add momentum to the zloty's rise later this week. \par \par \uc2  "If there was huge demand (at Wednesday's tenders) the zloty could get a boost, but I would suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would come in again and prevent any more gains," said Ryszard Bublik, dealer at Nomura International in London. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday the finance ministry offers one billion zlotys of two-and five-year T-bonds as well as 450 million zlotys of fixed rate notes, maturing on October 12, 2002. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expected the average yield of five-year T-bonds to inch up to around 19.0 percent from 18.7 percent at the auction in early March. The average yield of two-year notes was seen at 22.50 on Wednesday against 21.5 at the previous auction. \par \par \uc2  "Compared to the previous tender, yields should go slightly higher. Foreign demand at the auctions will probably be large, but I don't think investors will bid aggressively," said Marcin Rzechowski, fixed income dealer at Societe Generale in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  The average yield of four-year notes was expected to inch up to about 19.8 percent against 19.4 percent at a similar auction on March 4. \par \par \uc2  On the money market short-term rates inched down on the first day of the new monthly reserve period. Overnight rates closed at 23.90/24.25 against opening 24.40/24.75 percent. \par \par \uc2  Tomorrow/next ended at 24.00/24.45 against 24.40/24.80 at the start. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 118 million zlotys of 28-day NBP-bills on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  ((Pawel Kozlowski, Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3v08cpk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
