\par\par \b \uc2  Zloty eyes profit-taking dip after c. bank action. \b0\par\par\uc2 418 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, March 24 (Reuters) - Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will stay jittery on Wednesday and some players may book profits on the zloty's recent gains after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  unexpectedly bought dollars on the market on Tuesday, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time in over a month, buying an estimated $35 million when the zloty was between 6.7-6.2 percent above the parity rate. \par \par \uc2  The zloty closed on Tuesday at 6.26/6.22 percent above the parity rate against Monday's 6.76/6.70 percent close. \par \par \uc2  It closed at 3.4420/40 to the dollar against 3.4260/80 on Monday and at 1.8843/48 to the mark against 1.8737/50. \par \par \uc2  "It seems that investors received a clear signal that it's time to take profits. If players start closing positions we can see a major weakening of the zloty," said Zbigniew Chmielewski, treasurer at ING Barings in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  "Without the NBP's help, the zloty may even slide to around 5.0 percent from parity," Chmielewski added. \par \par \uc2  But other analysts said the zloty could find support much earlier. \par \par \uc2  "The market is looking at six percent as a pretty strong support level, so profit taking seems limited," said Matthew Vogel, economist at Donaldson, Lufkin & Jenrette. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Poland's strong fundamentals and lucrative yields on longer-term treasuries should help the zloty rebound from any dip. \par \par \uc2  "We could see some profit-taking, but it's going to be only temporarily. If nothing else negative happens, we will see investors coming in again," said Zsolt Papp, emerging markets economist at UBS in London. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Poland's secondary treasury market stayed calm on Tuesday. If the zloty weakened, fresh capital could flow into attractive fixed rate notes, they added. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to pull the zloty down to 6.0 percent, but the weaker the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is, the bigger foreign interest in our T-bonds becomes," said Wojciech Kowalczyk, senior fixed income dealer at Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  On the money market, short-term deposits held steady on Tuesday and overnight rates closed at 24.50/24.80 against the opening 24.45/24.70. Tomorrow/next ended at 24.60/24.85 against 24.55/24.80 at the start. ((Pawel Kozlowski, Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3o08t5p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
