\par\par \b \uc2  Sensitive zloty seen jittering on speculation. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pawel Kozlowski \par \uc2 547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, March 20 (Reuters) - Market speculation, which pushed the Polish zloty into wild swings this week, is likely to continue in the near term as players start to take profits on the unit's recent gains, analysts said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The zloty rocketed more than one percentage point on Friday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief told PAP news agency that she saw no weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the short term. \par \par \uc2  Hanna Gronkiewicz-Waltz said in an interview that the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the zloty approached the 10 percent level above the parity rate, remarks misinterpreted by the market as a go-ahead for a further zloty rise. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody knows where the upper limit of the band is, but as the market has been oversensitive, the words of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's head have had an incredible impact on the market," said Krzysztof Rybinski, chief analyst at ING Barings in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the zloty's sharp rise this week may prompt mass profit-taking and the market should find an equilibrium around six percent from the trading band centre next week. \par \par \uc2  "We can see the zloty moving back to six percent rather than staying at these levels where it is at the moment," said Ryszard Bublik, dealer at Nomura International in London. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this week, the zloty fell nearly one percentage point after U.S. investment bank Goldman Sachs said investors should take profits on Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and debt instruments. \par \par \uc2  "This Wednesday sent shivers throughout the market as any negative signal sparks incredible foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying," said Zbigniew Chmielewski, treasurer at ING Barings in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  "The market has been very sensitive since the zloty reached such dangerous levels," Chmielewski said. \par \par \uc2  The zloty set a new all-time high on Friday, ending the day at 7.26/6.99 percent from the trading band centre against last week's close of 5.86/5.65 percent. \par \par \uc2  The RPP decided on Wednesday to leave official interest rates unchanged. It also left the benchmark 28-day open market rate at the minimum level of 24 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the strong zloty should keep most foreigners away from Monday's one billion zloty primary T-bill auction, where yields are seen rising from the previous tender levels. \par \par \uc2  "If the zloty stays as strong as it is we won't see many foreign investors on Monday," said Wlodzimierz Kado, fixed income dealer at Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  "Yields should move higher as these papers are not very attractive if the money market remains cash-dry," Kado said. \par \par \uc2  Short-term rates traded on Friday above the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's minimum drainage rate of 24 percent as Polish banks continued to see cash shortages on their monthly accounts. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expected T-bill yields to inch up to secondary market levels at Monday's tender. The benchmark 52-week paper is expected to rise to about 22.80 percent from Monday's 22.30. \par \par \uc2  "The 13-week bill yield should also go up by about 10 to 20 basis points at the tender," Handlowy's Kado said. \par \par \uc2  ((Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du3k08vep\par }\page {\par