\par\par \b \uc2  EMERGING MARKETS-Zloty dliemma - shades of ERM past. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mike Dolan \par \uc2 735 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, March 13 (Reuters) - The policy dilemma surrounding the surge of Poland's zloty this year has cast analysts' minds back to the monetary conundrum faced by Spain and Portugal in the early 1990s. \par \par \uc2  Back then, those countries were dogged by a phenomenon known by market players as "excess credibility". \par \par \uc2  This was where a tidal wave of foreign capital swamped the domestic market and threatened to distort domestic monetary policy by undermining the fight against inflation. \par \par \uc2  If the analogy proves even partly correct, some analysts argue Polish markets could be in for a rocky ride when the current euphoria over its domestic markets ebbs. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing policy problems build up here, which are very similar to the 'excess credibility' story surrounding the high-yielders in the ERM in the early 1990s," said Avinash Persaud, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at JP Morgan. \par \par \uc2  "The Polish authorities will need to be very careful how they handle this, and investors will do well to be aware of the longer-term risks to a market in this position." \par \par \uc2  The zloty has risen to its highest level against the German mark in seven months and its highest against the dollar in four months. \par \par \uc2  More importantly, it is approaching the upper end of its permitted fluctuation range against a basket of target \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon be obliged to cap it via direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or lower interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Here lies the problem. \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inflation rate, at 13.2 percent year-on-year in January, is almost 12 percentage points higher than the European Union average. \par \par \uc2  As a result, it needs at least some nominal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation to maintain export competitiveness and avoid a balance of payments problem, and to get inflation down it needs to keep high interest rates. \par \par \uc2  With economic growth expected to remain brisk at 5.8 percent in 1998, the current account deficit is expected to widen to almost six percent of gross domestic product from 3.2 percent last year. \par \par \uc2  The situation is familiar. Emerging market players will look to last year's Czech crown devaluation as a model but more conventional investors may see more similarity with the European Union's recent history. \par \par \uc2  Spain's attempts to minimise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility via membership of the EU's exchange rate mechanism sent foreign investors scrambling for the high interest rates available on peseta securities in the early 1990s. \par \par \uc2  These flows put downward pressure on local interest rates and distorted domestic monetary policy which is aimed a cutting high inflation rates. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell pesetas simply pumped the local market full of pesetas and pushed interest rates lower prematurely. \par \par \uc2  The Spanish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reluctance to cut official interest rates in the face of persistently high inflation and strong domestic growth, meant the peseta was pinned for years to the upper limits of its permitted ERM bands. \par \par \uc2  This ultimately lead to a prolonged overvaluation of the peseta, exaggerated Spain's current account deficit and increased its dependence on foreign capital, much of which had been short-term. \par \par \uc2  The problem culminated in a series of peseta devaluations, following the ERM crisis of 1992. \par \par \uc2  "Poland is ultimately caught with conflicting inflation and exchange rate targets," said Zsolt Papp, emerging market economist at UBS. \par \par \uc2  "My hunch is they should concentrate on getting inflation below 10 percent and let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float if necessary," he said. \par \par \uc2  The country's expected membership of the EU within the next five or six years reinforces the attraction of locking into short-term yield of more than 20 percent. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, capital exchange controls on zloty trading and a officially-targeted and pre-announced monthly depreciation target for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , minimises \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk in a similar way to the ERM. \par \par \uc2  Full liberalisation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and a removal of the crawling peg may be the only solution. \par \par \uc2  "Poland is ready to adopt more flexible rules as far as capital account convertibility is concerned," Stanislaw Gomulka, an economist at the London School of Economics and an independent adviser to Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, said this week. \par \par \uc2  "But the key defence is elsewhere - the right macroeconomic \par \par \uc2   policy in general. In particular a low budget deficit...and also \par \par \uc2   structural changes," he said. \par \par \uc2     ((London Newsroom +44 171 542 6762, fax 583 7239, \par \par \uc2  uk.emergingmarkets.news@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du3d004dd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
