\par\par \b \uc2  POLISH ZLOTY STRENGTHENS AS C.BANK STAYS QUIET. \b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Februar 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Feb 23 (Reuters) - The zloty gained more ground on Monday as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  refrained from buying \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market for the second consecutive session and dealers said continued foreign flows would create more demand for the unit. \par \par \uc2  Over the past two weeks the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) stepped in with \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly every day so as to cap the foreign-charged local unit's rise. \par \par \uc2  "It is a roulette and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep the market guessing about its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said Rafal Petsch, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Bank of America Polska SA. \par \par \uc2  Last Friday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor said the NBP would try to fend off foreign short-term capital inflows by creating more uncertainty on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  "But as the NBP did not step in today the zloty will be stronger tomorrow as foreign demand for Polish T-bills and T-bonds continues," Bank of America's Petsch said. \par \par \uc2  The zloty ended Monday's session at 2.34/2.28 percent from the trading band centre against Friday's 2.23/2.20 close. \par \par \uc2  It closed at 3.5570/85 to the dollar against 3.5595/05 on Friday and at 1.9570/85 to the mark against 1.9568/73. \par \par \uc2  At Monday's one billion zloty primary auction foreign investors bought an estimated $120 million worth of Polish T-bills, whose minimum prices rose sharply amid a total demand of 3.57 billion zlotys. \par \par \uc2  The average yield of the benchmark 52-week T-bill, mostly sought by foreign investors, fell to 21.74 percent from 22.61 percent at the previous tender last Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Poland's new Monetary Policy Council (RPP), which plans to hold news conference after its Wednesday morning meeting, could announce some decisions to help keep Poland's markets in balance despite the ongoing foreign capital influx. \par \par \uc2  "Poland is perceived as a very good place for investment and if interest rates are high and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relatively cheap we can expect more capital flows in the near-term," one foreign bank dealer in Warsaw said. \par \par \uc2  On the money market one-day depos held relatively stable on Monday and overnight rates ended the session at 22.85/23.00 against opening 22.70/23.20. Tomorrow/next ended at 22.70/22.95 against Friday's close of 21.00/22.40. \par \par \uc2  ((Pawel Kozlowski, Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040130du2n00ll4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
