\par\par \b \uc2  POLISH C.BANK TALK LIFTS ZLOTY TO 7-WEEK HIGH. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Dec 16 (Reuters) - The Polish zloty jumped to a seven-week high on Tuesday on the latest National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at keeping the skittish local \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market just out-of-balance, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  In a Tuesday newspaper deputy NBP chief Witold Kozinski said the zloty's crawling devaluation could be cut as early as February if fiscal spending remained in check, giving the unit a shot in the arm and lifiting it nearly to the parity rate. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is sending the market mixed signals. The crawling peg cut is a positive factor, which offsets an earlier comment about widening the zloty's trading band and increasing risk," said Andrzej Ladko, chief dealer at Bank Handlowy SA. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday the zloty was fixed at 0.05 percent below the trading band centre -- the parity rate -- up from 0.87 percent on Monday and Friday's fixing at 1.16 percent below parity. \par \par \uc2  On the market it closed at 0.05/0.11 percent below parity or at 3.5340/60 to the dollar, against against 3.5505/20 on Monday and at 1.9830/40 per mark, against Monday's 2.0015/20. \par \par \uc2  Last week the NBP said it would soon expand the zloty's trading band to plus/minus 15 percent around the mid-point from the present seven percent on either side of parity, sending the zloty sharply lower. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said, however, that in the end it would be capital flows which would determine zloty sentiment and that they would be driven by Poland's fundementals and the authorities' ability to keep the growing current account deficit in check. \par \par \uc2  They said fund managers were now sitting on positions and waiting for the end of the year, especially if they fulfilled investment requirements for 1997, but that they would again look for places to invest their money early next year. \par \par \uc2  "I wouldn't now be surprised if there was an inflow in January and the zloty was pushed up above the parity, even far above the parity. Especially if the trading band was expanded," said Andrew Kenningham, analyst at Merrill Lynch in London. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that statements such as the International Monetary Fund's Tuesday assessment of Poland's current account defcit, which it saw under four percent of gross domestic product this year and under six percent in 1998, would also help keep the zloty strong in months to come. \par \par \uc2  Traders said that speculation by foreign players, which has recently played a leading large role on the thin Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, has slowed after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixed the zloty about half a percent above market level last week. \par \par \uc2  "The sentiment for the zloty is positive even though speculation has faded before the end of 1997 as Polish corporates are net zloty buyers in the late stages of the year," said Rafal Petsch, trader at Bank of America in London. \par \par \uc2  ((Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)) \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040131dtcg013g2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
